<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Troyes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1420">1420</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> was agreed on 21 May 1420 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry V of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>, backed by the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. It disinherited the Dauphin Charles and made Henry heir to the French throne, and Henry married Charles's daughter Catherine of Valois. Henry died before Charles in 1422, and the Dauphin, crowned as Charles VII in 1429, eventually drove the English from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes.jpg" alt="Treaty of Troyes" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ratification_du_Trait%C3%A9_de_Troyes_1_-_Archives_Nationales_-_AE-III-254.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>English kings had claimed the French throne since Edward III assumed the title in 1340. Henry V invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1415 and again in 1417, helped by the condition of Charles VI, who suffered bouts of mental illness from 1392 and whose intermittent incapacity encouraged rivalry among the royal family.</p><p>The murder of the king's brother Louis, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, in 1407 left a lasting division between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> and Armagnacs. After Henry conquered Normandy, talks in 1419 collapsed, and on 10 September 1419 John the Fearless was assassinated at Montereau by one of the Dauphin's attendants. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> then allied with Henry, and Henry now demanded the French crown itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was sealed in Troyes Cathedral on 21 May 1420, in the name of Charles VI, though Henry largely dictated its terms. Charles was to remain king for life, after which the crown would pass to Henry and his heirs, with the two kingdoms keeping their own laws and customs.</p><p>Henry was to be styled heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, to take over the government, and to marry Charles's daughter Catherine of Valois; the wedding took place on 2 June 1420. The Dauphin was disinherited and referred to only as &quot;Charles, calling himself dauphin of Viennois&quot;. The treaty avoided saying whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s right came from the English claim or from Charles's authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Estates General approved the treaty in December 1420, though it represented only those parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> willing to accept it, and its validity was questioned in France from the outset, since a king could not disinherit his son. Henry V died on 31 August 1422, less than two months before Charles VI, so his infant son Henry VI became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and, under the treaty, of France.</p><p>The Dauphin claimed the throne as Charles VII and was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 17 July 1429. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> broke with the English in 1435 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1435">Treaty of Arras</a>. The treaty was never formally rescinded, and English and British monarchs styled themselves kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> until 1801.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Troyes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Treaty of Troyes</b> he was regent and heir to the French throne, but his death in 1422 left the dual monarchy to his infant son.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Troyes</b> in 1420 made him heir to the French throne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Burgundy allied with England, leading to the <b>Treaty of Troyes</b> that made Henry V heir to France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-10">10 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
